    "fill": "The proposed criterion reduces admissible mechanisms to graph-generated affine and rank charts, reduces arbitrary source-law containment to finite proportional-column surjections, couples regimes through principal response matrices, and asks for an explicit certified compiler with a size-dependent bit bound. The inferential rung compares observable characteristic functions directly on a compact subclass."
  },
  "related_work": "Dai et al., Theorem 2, motivate a graph-generated equivalence object for latent cyclic linear non-Gaussian models, but that theorem concerns observational distribution sets under a zero-allowed support convention and does not settle the exact-support intervention problem. Zhou et al. encode hard-intervention invariances for latent directed acyclic graphs through augmented maximal ancestral graphs; their object is graphical constraint equivalence rather than equality of cyclic parametric response-kernel sets. Bongers et al. supply the solvability semantics that justify imposing invertibility separately after every intervention. Hyttinen, Eberhardt, and Hoyer give necessary and sufficient experiment-design conditions for full second-order recovery, which is a different target from the quotient under an arbitrary fixed intervention family. Sharifian et al., Proposition 5.1, recover row \\(i\\) of a square no-latent coefficient matrix from observational and atomic interventional distributions; Theorem 5 states the precise projection, lift, row-recovery map, and matching-set equality for the response-kernel semantics used here. Taeb et al. and Joint Causal Inference make shared mechanisms across environments explicit, but do not provide the required cyclic non-Gaussian common-lift theorem. Dadgostari and Nazemi study aligned response matrices for sensing an entirely latent equilibrium state and identify a single mechanism under target coverage or support conditions; they do not compare graph-indexed full-law sets under observed-node perfect interventions, arbitrary independent sources, and exact cancellations. Schkoda and Drton provide rank-based goodness-of-fit tests, while Wang, Kolar, and Drton provide test inversion for acyclic orderings; the statistical rung instead tests full observable law-set proximity for compact latent feedback models.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The affine and Hall--Rado realization criterion has passed forced-zero, latent-rank, degenerate-pivot, and exact rational witness checks at \\(N=4\\), but these finite checks do not prove the universal punctured-affine selection theorem. The source-partition argument still requires a full audit of finite-support measure equality in the original jump lattice and non-Gaussian closure after merging. The population compiler must handle every lower-dimensional cell and produce its own explicit complexity bound. The displayed strict-refinement witness does not establish failure of independent regime-wise equivalence; that stronger issue remains Open-ended Question 2.",
  "honest_scope": "The intervention pushforward identity, finite checks, and explicit witness provide proof architecture, not settled results. The sound-and-complete chart theorem, source-factorization theorem, effective compiler, common-lift boundary, no-latent row-lift identity, and statistical certification are all stated as claims to prove. The theorem is uniform over each finite input size but does not range over unbounded latent cardinality in one run. Under \\(\\ell=0\\), the observational projection equals the square perfect-matching object, and the atomic projection restricts it by the recovered row labels. No strict-extension corollary to Dai et al.'s zero-allowed observational class is asserted because that class permits coefficient-zero strata and omits the coupled intervention object, so its graph-indexed equivalence relation is not the same ordered model-class comparison.",
